  "response": "Adjudicating an in-hospital collapse is a matter of specifying the rival explanations, timing every exposure and every specimen, and then ranking the available tests by how sharply each separates one explanation from the other. Clinicians weigh bedside and laboratory discriminators in real time, while pathologists, working with tissue and with molecular assays, supply the findings that ante-mortem data could not deliver.\n\nAny defensible reconstruction begins with the primary record of the admission, whose entries are timed to the hour. Blood cultures were drawn from two separate sites before antibiotics were escalated, that step being the direct result of the fever, diffuse rash, and hypotension the patient developed on hospital day eight. Chart material of that kind is the only evidence a later reviewer can interrogate, since no retrospective account can supply timing that was never written down.\n\nTwo explanations compete for the collapse, and at least one of them holds, perhaps both: the abrupt deterioration was caused by an adverse reaction to an administered drug, or the abrupt deterioration was caused by an invasive infection. Peripheral eosinophil counts above 1.5 x 10^9/L support a drug hypersensitivity reaction, and a count in that range therefore indicates that an administered drug produced the collapse. Marked peripheral eosinophilia is a hallmark laboratory finding of bacterial sepsis; that hallmark status and the discriminating force of a count above 1.5 x 10^9/L for drug hypersensitivity cannot both be true. Serum procalcitonin, by contrast, is typically elevated in bacterial sepsis and low in drug hypersensitivity. A skin biopsy showing interface dermatitis with eosinophils supports a drug-induced etiology, which confirms that an adverse reaction to an administered drug caused the deterioration.\n\nThe suspect antibiotic was stopped on the day the patient deteriorated. Improvement after withdrawal of a suspect medication constitutes a positive dechallenge. Negative blood cultures definitively exclude bacterial infection as a cause of deterioration, a conclusion that cannot be squared with the capacity of broad-range 16S ribosomal RNA PCR on autopsy tissue to identify bacteria when cultures are sterile. The post-mortem examination came later, after that antibiotic had been withdrawn, and its result takes one of two forms: either the autopsy identified an infectious cause of death, or the autopsy found no infectious cause of death, one of the two being so and not the other.\n\nWhere molecular and histological results diverge from the clinical impression, the convention is to grade each strand of evidence by its specificity rather than to average them, since a finding that only one explanation can generate outweighs several that both explanations can produce. Causality assessment instruments formalize this by scoring temporal plausibility, competing causes, and the response to withdrawal and re-exposure, while the pathologist's report records what tissue can and cannot establish. The adjudicated verdict is then entered in the record together with its grounds, so that any later reader can identify the single observation that decided the case.",
